---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PhoneShot from '../components/PhoneShot.astro';
import WindowShot from '../components/WindowShot.astro';
import { DOWNLOADS, DESKTOP_GUIDE_URL, README_URL, REPO, SERVER_GUIDE_URL, url } from '../lib/site';
---

<Layout
  title="Self-host"
  path="self-host/"
  description="Run Open Record on your own network: the desktop app's bundled server, or the server from source, paired with your phone over Wi-Fi."
>
  <div class="container">
    <PageHeader
      title="Self-host"
      lead="Run the Open Record server on a machine you control and use it from your phone on the same Wi-Fi. Start with the desktop app; run it from source if you want the server on its own."
    />

    <div class="page-body">
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <div class="card__actions">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="#desktop">Desktop app</a>
          <a class="button" href="#run-from-source">Run from source</a>
          <a class="button button--ghost" href="#away-from-home">Away from home</a>
          <a class="button button--ghost" href="#accounts">Accounts (optional)</a>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <section id="desktop" aria-labelledby="desktop-title">
        <h2 id="desktop-title">Desktop app</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          The desktop app runs its own local server. Turn on phone access and your phone can use it over your home
          Wi-Fi. There is no server setup beyond a firewall rule and your Supabase project.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h3>Install the desktop app</h3>
            <p>
              Get the Windows or Linux installer. The installers are unsigned. macOS has no installer yet; build the
              desktop app from source.
            </p>
            <div class="card__actions">
              <a class="button button--primary" href={DOWNLOADS.linuxDeb} rel="noopener">Linux .deb (recommended)</a>
              <a class="button" href={DOWNLOADS.windows} rel="noopener">Windows installer</a>
              <a class="button" href={DOWNLOADS.linuxAppImage} rel="noopener">Linux AppImage</a>
              <a class="button button--ghost" href={url('install/')}>All downloads</a>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Allow phones on this network</h3>
            <p>
              Open <strong>Settings &gt; Desktop app</strong>, or go to <code>http://127.0.0.1:47900/desktop</code>.
              Turn on <strong>Allow phones on this network</strong>. The server restarts on your network address and
              shows a LAN address, a copy button and a QR code.
            </p>
            <p>Allow TCP 47900 from your own subnet only:</p>
            <code class="code-line">sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 47900 proto tcp</code>
            <p>
              On Windows, allow Open Record on the Private network. On macOS, allow incoming connections for Open Record
              in System Settings &gt; Network &gt; Firewall.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Pair your phone</h3>
            <p>
              Install the Android APK, or open the LAN address in the phone browser. In the app, go to
              <strong>Settings &gt; Stream server</strong>, then choose <strong>Scan QR code</strong> or type the
              address. The desktop server never shares its local token with phones, so the phone signs in with the same
              Supabase account as the desktop app. Set that up first: see <a href="#accounts">Accounts</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="card__actions">
              <a class="button" href={DOWNLOADS.android} rel="noopener">Android APK</a>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <WindowShot
          name="09-desktop-phones"
          alt="Open Record desktop app, Desktop app settings: Allow phones on this network is on, showing the LAN address http://192.168.1.20:47900 and a pairing QR code."
          caption="Desktop app with phones allowed. The phone needs this address and the same Supabase account."
        />
        <div class="gallery">
          <PhoneShot
            name="08-settings"
            alt="Phone Settings screen with the Stream server section: a Scan QR code button and a server URL field."
            label="Settings > Stream server"
            caption="Scan the QR code, or type the LAN address."
          />
        </div>
        <p class="meta">
          Full desktop guide: <a href={DESKTOP_GUIDE_URL} rel="noopener">docs/desktop.md on GitHub</a>, or
          <a href={url('desktop/')}>the desktop app page</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="run-from-source" aria-labelledby="source-title">
        <h2 id="source-title">Run from source</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Run the stream-link server on its own. It serves the web app and the API on one address, and it needs Node
          24 and yt-dlp on this machine.
        </p>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li><strong>Node 24</strong> and npm.</li>
          <li><strong>yt-dlp</strong>: <code>pipx install yt-dlp</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>ffmpeg</strong> (optional): only for transcoding while casting.</li>
        </ul>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h3>Clone and install</h3>
            <code class="code-line">{`git clone ${REPO}.git
cd open-record
npm ci`}</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Start the server</h3>
            <p>
              This builds the web app and the server, starts them detached, and prints the address and a pairing QR
              code. It binds your LAN address by default.
            </p>
            <code class="code-line">{`npm run server:up
npm run server:up -- --ip 192.168.1.20   # choose the LAN address
npm run server:up -- --stop              # stop the detached server
npm run server:dev                       # run in the foreground`}</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Connect a device</h3>
            <p>
              Open the printed <code>http://lan-ip:8787/</code> on any device on your Wi-Fi. For the Android app, build
              it with <code>npm run android</code> or install the APK, then use <strong>Settings &gt; Stream server</strong>
              and scan the QR code. For iPhone, follow <a href={url('iphone/')}>the iPhone build guide</a>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Keep it running</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          For an always-on host, write a systemd user unit that runs the built server instead of
          <code>server:up</code> (the server README has an example). Use <code>HOST=0.0.0.0</code> and allow
          only your subnet on port 8787:
        </p>
        <code class="code-line">sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 8787 proto tcp</code>
        <div class="card__actions">
          <a class="button button--ghost" href={SERVER_GUIDE_URL} rel="noopener">Server README</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lan-caveats" aria-labelledby="caveats-title">
        <h2 id="caveats-title">LAN caveats</h2>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li>Plain HTTP and a bearer token: use trusted Wi-Fi only. Anyone on the same network can read the traffic.</li>
          <li>Never port-forward 8787 or 47900, and never expose them to the internet.</li>
          <li>
            Phone and server must share a subnet. Guest networks, separate VLANs and AP isolation block the connection.
          </li>
          <li>
            The hosted https web app cannot call an http LAN server (mixed content). Use the page served by your server,
            or the Android app.
          </li>
          <li>Reserve a DHCP address for the computer, and keep it awake while you listen.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="away-from-home" aria-labelledby="away-title">
        <h2 id="away-title">Away from home</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Outside your own Wi-Fi, use HTTPS. The simplest private option is Tailscale: run
          <code>tailscale serve --bg 8787</code> on the server, then use the <code>https://</code> address it gives you.
          You can also put your own HTTPS reverse proxy in front of the server.
        </p>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li>
            Start the server with <code>npm run server:up -- --host 127.0.0.1</code> so only the proxy can reach it.
            Phones must be on your tailnet for the Tailscale route.
          </li>
          <li>Set <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> to the HTTPS address, so the banner and QR code show it.</li>
          <li>
            Cast speakers cannot reach a loopback-only server. Set <code>CAST_RELAY_PORT</code> for casting; see the
            server README.
          </li>
          <li>
            Set <code>TRUST_PROXY=127.0.0.1</code> only when the proxy runs on the same machine. Never set
            <code>TRUST_PROXY=true</code> while the port is reachable from your LAN.
          </li>
          <li>Still never port-forward the port. The server README has the full settings.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card__actions">
          <a class="button button--ghost" href={SERVER_GUIDE_URL} rel="noopener">Server README</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="accounts" aria-labelledby="accounts-title">
        <h2 id="accounts-title">Accounts and sync (optional)</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Sign-in, sync and shared playlists use a Supabase project that you create and own. Open Record hosts no
          accounts. Running the server from source does not require Supabase: its pairing QR carries the token. The
          desktop app's phone pairing does: phones sign in with the same Supabase account as the desktop app.
        </p>
        <div class="card__actions">
          <a class="button button--ghost" href={`${README_URL}#supabase-accounts-optional`} rel="noopener">
            Supabase setup in the README
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
